    "id" : 5,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 262.748, 678.023, 349.253, 690.604 ],
    "font" : "CMMI10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "dn,k = [tn]d(t)h(t)k,"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 6,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 71.641, 642.92, 539.999, 672.598 ],
    "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "where [tn] is an operator that takes the coefficient of the term tn, then, such a matrix is called a Riordan array and we denote it as R(d(t),h(t)). If R(d(t),h(t)) and R(g(t),f(t)) are Riordan arrays, the multiplication ∗ is defined as:"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 7,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 191.962, 625.053, 420.039, 642.338 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "R(d(t),h(t)) ∗ R(g(t),f(t)) := R(d(t)g(h(t)),f(h(t))"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 8,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 71.641, 596.276, 539.999, 625.955 ],
    "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "which is another Riordan array. Hence, the identity matrix in Riordan array form is R(1,t) while the inverse matrix of R(d(t),h(t)) is given as:"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 10,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 324.947, 568.28, 354.968, 592.172 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "1 d(h¯(t))"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
