    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "R(d(t),h(t))−1 := R"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 13,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 71.641, 535.559, 539.998, 559.368 ],
    "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "where h¯(t) is the compositional inverse of h(t), that is h(h¯(t)) = h¯(h(t)) = t. Therefore, the set of Riordan arrays forms a group called the Riordan group under multiplication."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 14,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 71.532, 508.261, 539.998, 531.174 ],
    "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "While Riordan arrays have various useful properties, we primarily utilize the following result called the fundamental theorem of Riordan arrays (FTRA):"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
